## Networking Devices – Basics: 

Today I learned about important networking devices: Modem, Ethernet Card (NIC), Hub, Switch, and Bridge.

## 1. Modem
A modem converts digital signals into analog signals and vice versa so a network can connect to the internet through an ISP.

Key Points:
- Connects network to the internet
- Required for internet access
- Works with routers

Cybersecurity Note:
Modems/routers can be targeted by attackers through firmware vulnerabilities.

---

## 2. Ethernet Card (NIC – Network Interface Card)
A NIC allows a computer or device to connect to a network. Every device must have a NIC to communicate.

Key Points:
- Has a unique MAC address
- Can be wired or wireless
- Enables network communication

Cybersecurity Note:
Used in MAC spoofing and packet sniffing attacks.

---

## 3. Hub
A hub is a basic networking device that sends data to all devices in the network.

Key Points:
- No intelligence
- Broadcasts data to everyone
- Causes collisions
- Mostly outdated

Cybersecurity Note:
Easy for attackers to sniff network traffic in hub-based networks.

---

## 4. Switch
A switch sends data only to the intended device using MAC addresses.

Key Points:
- Smarter than hub
- Reduces network traffic
- Used in modern networks
- Faster communication

Cybersecurity Note:
Vulnerable to MAC flooding and ARP spoofing.

---

## 5. Bridge
A bridge connects two networks and filters traffic between them.

Key Points:
- Connects network segments
- Reduces unnecessary traffic
- Works at data link layer

Cybersecurity Note:
Can be used in man-in-the-middle attack setups if compromised.

---

## Why This Matters in Cybersecurity
Understanding networking devices helps in:
- Packet analysis
- Network scanning
- Sniffing attacks
- ARP spoofing
- Man-in-the-middle attacks

Strong networking basics = strong cybersecurity foundation.
